Assuming the object is stored in a MongoDB collection named `objects`, and its financial valuation is stored in a field named `financialValuation` as a number in US dollars, you can use the following MongoDB query to filter and retrieve an object whose valuation exceeds **$10**:

```javascript
db.objects.findOne({
  financialValuation: { $gt: 10 }
});
```

If you want to return only the relevant fields, you can use a projection:

```javascript
db.objects.findOne({
  financialValuation: { $gt: 10 }
}, {
  _id: 0,
  name: 1,
  financialValuation: 1
});
```

If you want to retrieve multiple matching objects rather than just one, use `find` instead of `findOne`:

```javascript
db.objects.find({
  financialValuation: { $gt: 10 }
}, {
  _id: 0,
  name: 1,
  financialValuation: 1
});
```

Or, if you want only one matching object but with explicit limiting:

```javascript
db.objects.find({
  financialValuation: { $gt: 10 }
}, {
  _id: 0,
  name: 1,
  financialValuation: 1
}).limit(1);
```

Replace `objects` with your actual collection name, and replace `financialValuation` with the field name used in your database if it is different.

