Yes. In C++, the STL `std::map` does **not** have built-in persistence. To make it persistent, you normally **serialize its contents to a file** and then **read it back later**.

The easiest simple way is usually to write each key/value pair to a text file, then read the file back into a new `std::map`.

Below is a simple example.

---

## Example: persist `std::map<std::string, int>`

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <sstream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>

void saveMap(const std::map<std::string, int>& m, const std::string& filename)
{
    std::ofstream out(filename);

    if (!out) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Could not open file for writing: " + filename);
    }

    for (const auto& entry : m) {
        out << entry.first << '\t' << entry.second << '\n';
    }
}

std::map<std::string, int> loadMap(const std::string& filename)
{
    std::ifstream in(filename);

    if (!in) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Could not open file for reading: " + filename);
    }

    std::map<std::string, int> m;
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(in, line)) {
        if (line.empty()) {
            continue;
        }

        std::istringstream iss(line);

        std::string key;
        int value = 0;

        // Read key up to tab character
        if (!std::getline(iss, key, '\t')) {
            continue;
        }

        // Read integer value
        if (!(iss >> value)) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Invalid line in file: " + line);
        }

        m[key] = value;
    }

    return m;
}

int main()
{
    std::map<std::string, int> myMap;

    myMap["apples"] = 10;
    myMap["oranges"] = 5;
    myMap["bananas"] = 7;

    // Save map to file
    saveMap(myMap, "map_data.txt");

    // Later, load it back
    std::map<std::string, int> restoredMap = loadMap("map_data.txt");

    // Print restored map
    for (const auto& entry : restoredMap) {
        std::cout << entry.first << " = " << entry.second << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Example file content:

```text
apples	10
oranges	5
bananas	7
```

When the program runs again, it can restore the map from the file.

---

## Important notes

### 1. Choose a safe file format

The example above uses a simple text format:

```text
key<TAB>value
```

This is easy, but it assumes:

- keys do not contain tabs or newlines
- values can be read using `operator>>`

If your data is more complex, consider using **JSON**, CSV, SQLite, or another structured format.

---

### 2. For `std::map<std::string, std::string>`

If your map values are strings too, you can read them like this:

```cpp
void saveMap(const std::map<std::string, std::string>& m,
             const std::string& filename)
{
    std::ofstream out(filename);

    if (!out) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Could not open file for writing");
    }

    for (const auto& entry : m) {
        out << entry.first << '\t' << entry.second << '\n';
    }
}

std::map<std::string, std::string> loadMap(const std::string& filename)
{
    std::ifstream in(filename);

    if (!in) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Could not open file for reading");
    }

    std::map<std::string, std::string> m;
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(in, line)) {
        if (line.empty()) {
            continue;
        }

        std::istringstream iss(line);

        std::string key;
        std::string value;

        if (!std::getline(iss, key, '\t')) {
            continue;
        }

        if (!std::getline(iss, value)) {
            continue;
        }

        m[key] = value;
    }

    return m;
}
```

---

### 3. If your map contains custom objects

Suppose you have:

```cpp
struct Person
{
    std::string name;
    int age;
};
```

Then you cannot directly write a `Person` object to a file unless you serialize its fields.

Example:

```cpp
void savePerson(const Person& p, std::ofstream& out)
{
    out << p.name << ',' << p.age << '\n';
}
```

And load it back:

```cpp
Person loadPerson(std::ifstream& in)
{
    Person p;
    std::string line;

    if (!std::getline(in, line)) {
        return p;
    }

    std::istringstream iss(line);
    std::string name;
    int age = 0;

    std::getline(iss, name, ',');
    iss >> age;

    p.name = name;
    p.age = age;

    return p;
}
```

Then your map could be:

```cpp
std::map<std::string, Person> people;
```

You would write:

```cpp
for (const auto& entry : people) {
    out << entry.first << '\t';
    savePerson(entry.second, out);
}
```

The key idea is: **convert your data into a portable representation**, write it to a file, then convert it back later.

---

## Easier alternative: use JSON

If you want something more robust and easier for many types, use a JSON library.

For example, with [nlohmann/json](https://github.com/nlohmann/json), which is a popular header-only C++ JSON library:

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>

using json = nlohmann::json;

void saveMap(const std::map<std::string, int>& m, const std::string& filename)
{
    json j;

    for (const auto& entry : m) {
        j[entry.first] = entry.second;
    }

    std::ofstream out(filename);
    out << j.dump();
}

std::map<std::string, int> loadMap(const std::string& filename)
{
    std::ifstream in(filename);

    json j = json::parse(in);

    std::map<std::string, int> m;

    for (auto it = j.begin(); it != j.end(); ++it) {
        m[it.key()] = it.value().get<int>();
    }

    return m;
}

int main()
{
    std::map<std::string, int> myMap;

    myMap["apples"] = 10;
    myMap["oranges"] = 5;

    saveMap(myMap, "map_data.json");

    std::map<std::string, int> restored = loadMap("map_data.json");

    for (const auto& entry : restored) {
        std::cout << entry.first << " = " << entry.second << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Example JSON file:

```json
{
  "apples": 10,
  "oranges": 5
}
```

JSON is often a better choice because it handles escaping, strings, numbers, booleans, nested structures, etc. more safely than a simple custom text format.

---

## What about binary files?

You can also write binary data, but it is usually **not recommended** for simple persistence because binary files can be:

- non-portable between compilers/platforms
- affected by endianness
- affected by padding and memory layout
- hard to debug
- sensitive to C++ type changes

For example, writing raw `int` or `double` values may work on your machine, but may not work on another machine or compiler.

For simplicity and safety, prefer:

1. plain text format, or  
2. JSON format.

---

## Things to remember

### `std::map` is not automatically persistent

This will not magically save the map:

```cpp
std::map<std::string, int> m;
m["key"] = 42;
```

When the program ends, the map usually disappears unless you write it to disk.

You need explicit save/load functions.

---

### Do not store non-serializable values

Avoid storing things like:

```cpp
FILE*
std::unique_ptr<Something>
file handles
sockets
database connections
```

in the map if you want to save/load it. These are runtime resources, not plain persistent data.

Instead, store plain data:

```cpp
std::string
int
double
bool
struct with simple fields
```

---

### If you use `std::unordered_map`

The same idea works:

```cpp
std::unordered_map<std::string, int> m;
```

You can save and load key/value pairs the same way.

However, note that `std::unordered_map` does not guarantee iteration order. If you need to preserve insertion order exactly, you may need to store an explicit order, for example using a separate `std::vector<std::string>` of keys.

---

## Simple recommendation

If you just want a quick and easy solution:

```cpp
std::map<std::string, int>
```

then write each pair as text:

```cpp
out << key << '\t' << value << '\n';
```

and read it back with streams.

If you want something more robust and maintainable, use **JSON**, for example with `nlohmann/json` or `Boost.JSON`.

In short:

> To make an STL `std::map` persistent, serialize its contents to a file on save, and reconstruct a new `std::map` from the file when the program runs again.

