```java
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

public class Main {
    private static final String PHRASE = "Hello from a multithreaded world!";
    private static final int THREAD_COUNT = 5;

    // Lock used to ensure only one thread can access printPhrase() at a time.
    private static final Object PRINT_LOCK = new Object();

    /**
     * Printing method. Only one thread can execute this method at any given time
     * because the method synchronizes on PRINT_LOCK.
     */
    public static void printPhrase() {
        synchronized (PRINT_LOCK) {
            System.out.println(PHRASE);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread[] threads = new Thread[THREAD_COUNT];

        for (int i = 0; i < THREAD_COUNT; i++) {
            threads[i] = new Thread(() -> {
                try {
                    // Random sleep between 0 and 2 seconds.
                    int sleepMillis = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(0, 2001);
                    Thread.sleep(sleepMillis);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    return;
                }

                // Each thread prints the phrase once.
                printPhrase();
            });

            threads[i].start();
        }

        // Wait for all threads to finish.
        for (Thread thread : threads) {
            thread.join();
        }

        System.out.println("All threads completed.");
    }
}
```

This program:

- Creates **5 threads**.
- Each thread sleeps for a **random interval between 0 and 2 seconds**.
- Calls `printPhrase()`.
- Uses a **synchronized lock** so only one thread can access the printing method at a time.
