To devise a robust CSS selector strategy that meets your requirements, we must first address a fundamental HTML/CSS reality: **HTML does not inherently understand "secondary" or "tertiary" `<div>` elements.** To target them reliably and maintainably, we must either use **structural pseudo-classes** (like `:nth-child`) or, preferably, **semantic attribute selectors** (like `data-tier`). 

Using `data-tier` is the industry best practice because it decouples the visual styling from the DOM structure, making the code highly maintainable. Below is the comprehensive CSS strategy and the rationale behind it.

### 1. The HTML Assumption
For this strategy to work, the secondary and tertiary containers should be marked up explicitly. This is the most maintainable approach:

```html
<!-- Secondary Container -->
<div class="container" data-tier="secondary">
  <p class="child-element">Secondary Child</p>
  <div class="nested-wrapper">
    <span class="deep-child">Tertiary Child</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Tertiary Container -->
<div class="container" data-tier="tertiary">
  <p class="child-element">Tertiary Child</p>
  <!-- May or may not have nested elements -->
</div>
```

*(Note: If you absolutely cannot alter the HTML, you would use `div.container:nth-child(2)` and `div.container:nth-child(3)`. However, this is brittle. The `data-tier` approach is strongly preferred).*

---

### 2. The CSS Selector Strategy

We will use the `:is()` pseudo-class to group selectors efficiently. This reduces code repetition and improves readability. We will also use CSS Custom Properties (Variables) for maintainability.

```css
/* ==========================================
   1. SETUP & VARIABLES (Best Practice)
   ========================================== */
:root {
  /* Base styles */
  --container-bg-secondary: #e3f2fd;
  --container-bg-tertiary: #ede7f6;
  --container-border: #90a4ae;

  /* State styles */
  --container-bg-hover: #bbdefb;
  --container-bg-active: #90caf9;
  --container-bg-visited: #ce93d8; /* Usually reserved for links, but styled here per requirement */
  --container-bg-focus: #ffd54f; /* High visibility for keyboard users */

  /* Child element styles */
  --child-text-secondary: #0d47a1;
  --child-text-tertiary: #4a148c;
  --nested-bg: #fff3e0;
}

/* ==========================================
   2. BASE STATE: Target Secondary & Tertiary Containers
   ========================================== */
/* Using :is() to combine selectors cleanly. 
   This is highly maintainable and efficient. */
:is(div.container[data-tier="secondary"], div.container[data-tier="tertiary"]) {
  padding: 20px;
  margin: 10px 0;
  border: 1px solid var(--container-border);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background-color: var(--container-bg-secondary); /* Default fallback */
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease, border-color 0.3s ease; /* Smooth state transitions */
}

/* Differentiate base styling between the two tiers */
div.container[data-tier="secondary"] {
  background-color: var(--container-bg-secondary);
}
div.container[data-tier="tertiary"] {
  background-color: var(--container-bg-tertiary);
}

/* ==========================================
   3. STATE MANAGEMENT (Hover, Active, Visited, Focus)
   ========================================== */
/* CRITICAL BEST PRACTICE: Order matters! 
   If a user hovers and clicks simultaneously, the :active state should win.
   Therefore, :hover must come BEFORE :active in the CSS cascade. */

/* Hover State */
:is(div.container[data-tier="secondary"], div.container[data-tier="tertiary"]):hover {
  background-color: var(--container-bg-hover);
  border-color: #546e7a;
}

/* Focus State (Best Practice for accessibility - keyboard navigation) */
:is(div.container[data-tier="secondary"], div.container[data-tier="tertiary"]):focus-visible {
  background-color: var(--container-bg-focus);
  outline: 2px dashed #d32f2f;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* Active State (Click) - Placed AFTER hover so it takes precedence */
:is(div.container[data-tier="secondary"], div.container[data-tier="tertiary"]):active {
  background-color: var(--container-bg-active);
  border-color: #1565c0;
  transform: scale(0.99); /* Subtle feedback */
}

/* Visited State 
   NOTE: Browsers typically only apply :visited to anchor tags (<a>). 
   To make a div respond to :visited, it must act as a link (e.g., <div role="link" href="#">).
   We style it anyway to fulfill the prompt's requirement. */
:is(div.container[data-tier="secondary"], div.container[data-tier="tertiary"]):visited {
  background-color: var(--container-bg-visited);
  border-color: #6a1b9a;
}

/* ==========================================
   4. NESTED ELEMENTS (Without affecting parents)
   ========================================== */
/* The requirement states we must style child elements uniquely 
   without affecting the parents. We achieve this using combinators. */

/* Direct Children: Styles <p>, <div>, etc., directly inside the container */
:is(div.container[data-tier="secondary"], div.container[data-tier="tertiary"]) > * {
  color: inherit;
  padding: 10px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
}

/* Specific Nested Children: Styles specific classes inside the containers */
div.container[data-tier="secondary"] .child-element {
  color: var(--child-text-secondary);
  font-weight: bold;
}

div.container[data-tier="tertiary"] .child-element {
  color: var(--child-text-tertiary);
  font-weight: normal;
}

/* Deeply Nested Elements: Styles elements nested 2+ levels deep */
div.container[data-tier="secondary"] .nested-wrapper {
  background-color: var(--nested-bg);
  padding: 5px;
}

div.container[data-tier="tertiary"] .nested-wrapper {
  background-color: #f3e5f5; /* Different nested style for tertiary */
}

div.container[data-tier="secondary"] .deep-child,
div.container[data-tier="tertiary"] .deep-child {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 2px 8px;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
```

