    println!("{}", factorial(5)); // 120
}
```

If you want to handle negative numbers safely, since JavaScript would not naturally stop for negative inputs, you can use a signed integer type and add a check:

```rust
fn factorial(n: i64) -> Result<i64, &'static str> {
    if n < 0 {
        return Err("factorial is not defined for negative numbers");
    }

    if n == 0 {
        return Ok(1);
    }

    Ok(n * factorial(n - 1)?)
}
```

Example:

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("{}", factorial(5).unwrap()); // 120
}
```

For very large factorials, consider using a big integer library such as `num-bigint`, because normal integer types can overflow.

